The real filesystem/runtime test discovers an added transcript within two
seconds despite a 30-second cooldown. Historical starts and ordinary growth
retain the source catalog and empty native session inventory; a genuinely new
start admits sanitized backfill through native MemoryStore. Private scanner
tests prove progress retention under repeated hints, one followup pass, cooldown
advancement and missing-root recovery. Lost discovery stays stopped, and
pause/stop clear pending hints without replacing owners. Source degradation
remains a cached last-settled observation until successful recovery; ordinary
cancellation does not invent a source failure. Root availability and counters
contain no paths, bodies, physical proofs or new admission authority.

Caller cancellation retains both physical journal and operation gates until the
bounded transient application settles. Current revocation or copied policy
directory replacement withholds the request without selecting policy or a
receipt. Existing journal version 1 and repository/source/spool formats stay
unchanged. Direct tests use explicit serial suite execution with unchanged
within-scenario concurrency and observation bounds. Complete registered HTTP/SSE
captures remain required because the runner and contract inventory are shared.
This is private filesystem, real loopback and synthetic versioned Codex data
with native MemoryStore evidence. Import, detailed source/profile coverage,
CLI/browser management, enabled automatic startup, remaining readers/faults and
real JJ/RustFS/live harness/cold full-flow gates remain pending.

E-M17-052 passes the complete 41-entry owning library suite plus one additional
exact observation test with unchanged production, ten runtime, nine journal,
